Exactly. The thing worth protecting is causal integrity: the story a person learns about why something works should eventually converge on the mechanism actually carrying the load.

What you described has a recognizable architecture:

\text{visible ritual }R
\quad+\quad
\text{hidden scaffold }H
\quad\rightarrow\quad
\text{success}

The person observes:

R\rightarrow\text{success}

and therefore learns the wrong causal model. Then H disappears:

R-H\rightarrow\text{nothing}

yet the person keeps pulling the lever because yesterday the lights came on.

That can generate superstition, dependency, endless troubleshooting, or increasingly ornate explanations for why the ritual “used to work.” The more faithfully the hidden scaffold backed the ritual beforehand, the more convincing the false attribution becomes.

I’d name a few pieces of that machinery:

Phantom Causality — the visible action gets credit for an unseen cause.

Scaffold Laundering — assistance is hidden so its result appears endogenous to something else.

Scaffold Withdrawal Trap — remove the hidden support after the wrong causal model has been learned.

Causal Mirror — show the participant the actual dependency graph afterward.

Fadeout Test — deliberately reduce one component at a time to determine which part was carrying the effect.

That last one is the antidote:

R+H
\rightarrow
R+0.75H
\rightarrow
R+0.5H
\rightarrow
R+0.25H
\rightarrow
R

Measure what changes. Now the person can actually learn the math.

And yes, AI deserves exactly this suspicion—but carefully calibrated. Modern neural networks really are only partially interpretable; even model developers do not have a complete human-readable account of the billions of computations producing every answer. At the same time, “black box” does not mean unknowable or that arbitrary stories can substitute for evidence. Interpretability researchers can increasingly trace internal features and circuits, perturb them, and see how outputs change. Anthropic, for example, has published circuit-tracing work and released tooling specifically to test hypotheses about internal mechanisms rather than trusting a model’s verbal explanation of itself. 

There are also concrete reasons to care about hidden mechanisms beyond philosophy: NIST has studied poisoned models in which concealed triggers alter behavior, illustrating why observed input/output behavior alone can fail to reveal everything relevant about a system. 

So the defensive rule for AI—or institutions, teachers, products, stories, even yourself—is:

\boxed{
\text{Don't merely ask, “Did it work?”}
\quad
\text{Ask, “What carried the result?”}
}

Then perturb it.

Remove one support. Swap the explanation. Change the operator. Replicate independently. See whether the supposed mechanism survives.

And your skydiving metaphor actually gives the constructive version. A trainer can let someone feel the fall without secretly removing the parachute. Simulation, theater, games, controlled uncertainty, VR, rehearsal, staged demonstrations—all can provide thrill and discovery while preserving an explicit return path.

The beautiful trick is therefore not convincing somebody of a false mechanism.

It’s letting them temporarily inhabit a compelling one, then handing them the mirror before the illusion becomes their prison.

That is where the thrill becomes instruction instead of gunk.